            2. How Much Power a Campsite Actually Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Add up the loads you would run at the same time, not everything you own. Phone and camera charging is 10 to 30 watts per device. A laptop is commonly 45 to 100 watts. LED string lights are typically well under 50 watts. A 12-volt compressor fridge averages tens of watts once it is cold. A CPAP without a heated humidifier often draws 30 to 60 watts, more with the heater on. None of these come close to taxing even a 1,000-watt generator.
          </p>

          <p>
            The loads that size the generator are the heating ones. An electric kettle, a drip coffee maker, a hot plate, an induction burner or a small space heater commonly draws 800 to 1,800 watts while it runs. Running one of them plus your charging loads is what pushes a campsite into the 2,000-watt class. If you never plan to boil water electrically, a 1,000-watt generator is genuinely enough, and it is lighter and quieter than anything bigger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Motor loads need start headroom. A household mini fridge, a small air conditioner or a sump-style pump briefly pulls several times its running power when it starts. Our guide to <a href="/best-portable-air-conditioner-for-camping/">portable air conditioners for camping</a> covers which units small generators can actually start. A 12-volt fridge is the exception: compressor fridges designed for vehicles start gently, which is why they pair so well with batteries. Our <a href="/best-12v-fridge-for-camping/">12V camping fridge picks</a> go into their power draw in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, campers sort into three groups. Charging, lights and a 12-volt fridge only: buy a power station or a 1,000-watt generator. Add a kettle, coffee maker or induction burner: buy a 2,000 to 2,500-watt inverter. Add a small air conditioner or a travel trailer: you are now shopping for RV power, and our <a href="/best-rv-generator/">RV generator guide</a> is the better place to start, because rooftop air conditioning changes the math entirely.
          </p>

