        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying more generator than the campsite needs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Campers often buy 3,000 or 4,000 watts for loads that total a few hundred. Every extra 500 watts adds weight, noise and fuel. Add up what you will run at the same time; unless you cook electrically or run an air conditioner, a 1,000 to 2,200-watt inverter is plenty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the generator near the tent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Placing a generator beside a tent, under a canopy or close to a car with sleeping occupants lets carbon monoxide build up. Keep it outdoors, at least 20 feet from occupied spaces where possible, exhaust pointed away, with automatic CO shutoff and a battery CO alarm where people sleep.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming generators are allowed everywhere</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many campgrounds restrict generator hours, ban them in tent or walk-in loops, or cap noise at 60 dB(A) at 50 feet. Showing up without checking can mean a weekend with no power at all. Read the campground page or call the ranger station before you pack the generator.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing the generator with untreated fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gasoline left in a small carburetor for months turns to varnish and is the most common reason camping generators will not start on the first trip of the season. Use stabilized fuel, run the carburetor dry before storage where the manual says to, and start it every month or two.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an open-frame generator to save money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cheap open-frame generators are loud enough to break campground noise limits and anger neighbors, heavier than small inverters, and their power is less suited to sensitive electronics. For camping, a budget inverter like the WEN costs little more and is far more welcome at the site.
              </p>
            </div>
