        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size generator do I need for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For charging phones and laptops, lights, fans and a 12-volt fridge, a 1,000-watt inverter is enough. If you want to run an electric kettle, coffee maker, hot plate or induction burner, step up to a 2,000 to 2,500-watt inverter. Only travel trailers with air conditioning need more, and that becomes an RV generator question with different sizing rules.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How quiet is a quiet camping generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Quality small inverters are rated in the high 40s to low 50s dB(A) at quarter load, measured from about 23 feet, which is roughly the level of a quiet conversation. They get several decibels louder at full load and louder still up close. Open-frame generators commonly run in the 70s, which is why many campgrounds ban them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a generator overnight at a campground?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Most campgrounds enforce quiet hours from around 10 p.m. to the morning, and many state and national parks restrict generators to set daytime windows. It is also a carbon monoxide risk near sleeping people. Run overnight loads like a CPAP or fridge from a battery power station, and recharge it with the generator during allowed hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a power station better than a generator for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For light loads and weekend trips, usually yes. A power station is silent, fume-free and allowed during quiet hours, and it runs phones, lights, a 12-volt fridge and a CPAP easily. A generator wins for electric cooking, long trips without sun and higher power. Many campers carry both, using the generator to refill the battery during allowed hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a small generator run on a tank of gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends mostly on load. Honda quotes the EU2200i at roughly 3.2 hours on its 0.95-gallon tank at rated load and about 8 hours at a quarter load. Typical camping loads are light, so a full tank often covers an evening and a morning. Generators with larger tanks, like the Yamaha EF2200iS, stretch that further.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a camping generator work as a mini generator for home outages?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, within limits. A 2,000-watt inverter can run a refrigerator, internet equipment, lights and phone charging during an outage, from outdoors and well away from doors and windows. It will not run central air, an electric range or a well pump. For whole-house needs, a larger portable with a transfer switch or a standby generator is the right tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a generator safe to use at a tailgate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, with care. Keep it outdoors and away from crowds, tents and canopies, with the exhaust pointed away from people and vehicles. Choose a quiet inverter with automatic CO shutoff, check the stadium lot rules, and let it cool before refueling. Never refuel a hot generator next to a grill or open flame.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
